<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/intermediate/judaism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Judaism is <strong>the covenant tradition: a 3,000-year relationship between a people and
    God, lived through law, text and argument</strong>. Its modern shape was forged by catastrophe
    and adaptation — temple to text after 70 CE, diaspora communities from Spain to Baghdad to
    Brooklyn — and its method (endless reasoned debate on sacred text) shaped its people's
    intellectual culture as much as its beliefs.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="covenant, text, law, and the streams" widget="equationStep" config={judaCfg} />

  <h2>What the study emphasises</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>History anchored by evidence:</strong> the Merneptah Stele (~1208 BCE) is the first extra-biblical mention of Israel; archaeology confirms and complicates the biblical narratives in instructive ways.</li>
    <li><strong>Exile and resilience:</strong> destructions (586 BCE, 70 CE) produced reinvention rather than disappearance — the template of a text-centred portable peoplehood, arguably one of history's most successful survival strategies.</li>
    <li><strong>The Talmud as method:</strong> the page preserves disagreement permanently — majority view, minority view, and the argument structure itself. Legal reasoning as sacred act; a distinctive contribution to world intellectual history.</li>
    <li><strong>Antisemitism as historical constant:</strong> from expulsions to pogroms to the Holocaust (six million murdered) — the study of Judaism includes the history of its persecutions, and their ongoing afterlife.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the Mishnah and Talmud (trans. editions); Holtz, Back to the Sources; the Merneptah Stele (Egyptian Museum, Cairo).</p>
</TopicPage>
